1. e4 e5 2. Bc4 { A romantic move with many fresh ideas for the attacking player.
Strategy: The setup is simple, we point our pawn chain to the kingside with d3
and then start charging with f4. Note the similarities to a King's Indian -
Instead of d6-e5 and then f5 we have d3-e4 and then f4. Note also that in
contrast to a Kings Indian our bishop is outside the pawn chain and that we get
our f4 break with much less fuss. Let's also take a moment to think about the
dreaded London system: the Bishop's opening is a mirror image of the London $1
First, notice how the pointing of the pawns changes the flavor of the game
entirley. We will be attacking the kingside and the king. In the traditional
London white aims for a nagging yank on the queenside. Like with the London
though, a key art of our play will be how we deal with black harassing our
bishop on c4 (in the London the bishop is of course of f4). We will need to be
creative and flexible. Like with the London it's important to undertand that
while our Bc4 looks pretty, our dark square bishop is our best piece. Bc1 moves
easily through our pawn chain. He is the mortar and the pawns are the bricks.
Making things easy: The Bishop's opening avoids the dreariness of names like
Petroff, Berlin and Marshall. It's much less known and explored than traditional
2. Nf3 lines. After learning a couple forcing lines the white player should
expect to develop a general feel as to where the pieces go. We use the same pawn
structure (d3-e4 with a later f4) - with our light square bishop outside the
chain - against the Sicilian. So we are aiming for a familiarity with early
middlegame attacking setups and dynamics. Style: Expect violence. The bishop's
opening, and every other opening in our aggressive repertoire, is not designed
to give us some small advantage that only the computer understands. The
repertoire aims to create an attack, with extreme danger and lack of balance for
both sides. } ({ clickable variation This move will transpose, so the practical
player might wish to mix it up with this move order and the dogmatist might wish
to play it on principle (\"knights before bishops $1\"). } 2. Nc3) 2... Nf6 (2... Bc5 { This move will transpose to either 2...Nc6 or 2...Nf6. }) (2... Nc6 3. Nc3 Bc5 { Nf6 will transpose to lines with 2...Nf6 } 4. Qg4 $1 { This position is highly tactical
